;; Upstream provenance catalog — which external source each part of this
;; repo mirrors, with the URL that was actually fetched.
;;
;; README.md says this library is "LangChain-core-style" and reproduces
;; "the same layering as upstream langchain-core / langgraph", and
;; docs/adr/0001-architecture.md carries an eight-row correspondence
;; table whose left column names upstream constructs. Until this file,
;; not one of those names had an address: nothing in the repo said WHICH
;; document a namespace is a port of, so a reader could not check whether
;; the port still matches its source, and could not tell an intentional
;; divergence from a stale one.
;;
;; Discipline (enforced by test/langchain/catalog_test.cljk):
;;   - :url was fetched on :verified and the FINAL response was :http.
;;     Redirects are resolved: a source is recorded at its terminal
;;     address, not at the historical one that still points there.
;;   - :mirrors are paths in this repo, and they must exist. A citation
;;     pointing at a file we do not have is provenance for nothing.
;;   - URLs are distinct. The same source listed twice is padding.
;;
;; That last rule did real work when this file was written. The upstream
;; per-concept pages (/docs/concepts/lcel/, /runnables/, /messages/,
;; /prompt_templates/, /tools/, /tool_calling/, /output_parsers/,
;; /chat_history/, /structured_outputs/) all still answer 200 — and nine
;; of them now terminate at ONE page, the OSS Python overview. Recorded
;; at their historical addresses they would have looked like nine
;; citations; resolved, they are one. The concepts that do still have
;; their own page are listed at it, and the rest are folded into the
;; single overview row below.
;;
;; The suite runs with no network by design, so :http here is the
;; recorded measurement, not a live one. What the tests DO check live is
;; everything this repo can answer about itself.
{:catalog/id :langchain/upstream-provenance
 :catalog/verified "2026-09-01"
